!requires-fakes
# SYNTHESIZED. The one tool registered `long_running=True` answers from its own
# worker thread, and its response is an ordinary tool result -- the transport
# does not leak which thread produced it. The fake `review` here returns
# immediately; the busy path, the read loop's liveness while it runs, and the
# EOF join are pinned in `tests/test_mcpserver.py`, where a test can hold the
# handler still. A transcript cannot: two responses in flight have no
# deterministic order on stdout.
> {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-11-25"}}
< {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 1, "result": {"protocolVersion": "2025-11-25"}}
> {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"notifications/initialized"}
> {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"review","arguments":{}}}
< {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 2, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": {"$contains": "reviewed"}}], "isError": false, "structuredContent": {"status": 0}}}
